"""SEN0401 chapter 8 (Digital Signatures), version 1.2.0: eighteen worked examples whose numbers a reader can change, with their teaching paragraphs.
Each leaf is (top, mid, leaf, example, definition, code, expected output, paragraph). The code is self-contained (standard library only, no double quote, whole-number literals the page can vary),
is executed by sen0401_rdodi_checks and its repr must equal the expected output; the passages each paragraph relies on are checked in the saved sources by the claims of sen0401_ch08_rdodi_data_v1_2_0.
The toy examples work in a stand-in group: the integers modulo a small number n, with the 'point' g * x written as g * x % n, so that the equations of the book hold line for line and can be checked by hand;
a real curve point is not an integer, so these examples show the algebra and not the security (the chapter's other examples run the real secp256k1)."""
__version__ = "1.2.0"
NEW = [
 ("SignatureFoundations", "GroupArithmetic", "ModularRemainder", "5 divided by 3 leaves 2",
  "Reducing a number modulo m keeps only the remainder of its division by m, so 5 modulo 3 is 2, which is the book's own example of what modulus means.",
  "a = 5; m = 3; (a % m, a // m)", "(2, 1)",
  "Every equation of the chapter is computed modulo a number, and the book explains the idea with one sentence: if the modulus were 3 and an operation gave 5, the number actually used is 2, because 5 divided by 3 has a remainder of 2 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The example computes that remainder with the percent operator and the whole quotient with the double slash, giving the pair (2, 1), and the reader can change the number and the modulus to see the remainder wrap around like the hand of a clock that returns to its start after twelve hours. In Bitcoin the modulus is not 3 but a prime of 256 bits for coordinates and the order of the group for scalars, and the next examples use a modulus of 101 so that every step can still be checked by hand."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "EcdsaMath", "ToyEcdsaCheck", "sign and verify with ECDSA's equations modulo 101",
  "With a stand-in group of integers modulo 101, the ECDSA equation s = k^-1 (z + r d) gives a signature that the verifier's combination of z/s and r/s accepts.",
  "n = 101; g = 7; d = 17; k = 29; z = 11; q = d * g % n; r = k * g % n; s = pow(k, -1, n) * (z + r * d) % n; w = pow(s, -1, n); (s, (z * w * g + r * w * q) % n == r)", "(95, True)",
  "The book gives the signing equation of ECDSA as s = k^-1 (Hash(m) + x times R), where k is the private nonce, R is the x coordinate of the public nonce and x is the private key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the example the private key d is 17, the nonce k is 29, the digest z is 11 and the group is the integers modulo 101 with the generator 7, so the public key is q = 17 times 7 modulo 101, the nonce point is r = 29 times 7 modulo 101 and the signature value is s = 95; the verifier computes w as the inverse of s, forms z times w times g plus r times w times q, and gets back r, which is the answer True. It is like a lock and key cut from one blank: the verifier never sees d or k, yet the numbers fit only when both were used together. Because ordinary multiplication stands in for the multiplication of curve points, the algebra is exact but the secrecy is not, since anyone can divide; a real curve has no such division, and that is where the security lies."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "EcdsaMath", "OtherValidS", "s and n - s both verify, only one is low",
  "If s is a valid ECDSA value then n - s is valid too, and exactly one of the two lies in the lower half of the range that BIP146 asks for.",
  "n = 101; s = 30; (n - s, s <= n // 2, n - s <= n // 2)", "(71, True, False)",
  "ECDSA signatures are malleable because the verification equation treats s and its negative modulo the group order n alike, so a third party can turn a valid signature into another valid one without knowing any key (Wuille et al., 2020). BIP146 answers this for scripts by requiring that the S value be at most the curve order divided by 2, the lower half of its range (Lau and Wuille, 2016). With the stand-in order 101 and s equal to 30, the partner value is 71, and the test s at most n divided by 2 is True for 30 and False for 71, so exactly one of the pair passes; the reader can change s to see that the halves swap at 51. It is like a coin with two faces that both name the same owner: the rule only fixes which face a signature must show. The real order has 256 bits, but the rule is the same comparison, and in Bitcoin Core it is a relay policy, which the next examples of the chapter separate from consensus."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "DerEncoding", "DerPadding", "a 33-byte R makes a 71-byte DER body",
  "A DER integer is signed, so an R whose first byte is 0x88 or higher needs a leading zero byte, which makes the book's signature 71 bytes before the hash type byte and 72 with it.",
  "rtop = 136; rlen = 32; slen = 32; pad = int(rtop >= 128); body = 2 + rlen + pad + 2 + slen; (pad, body + 2, body + 3)", "(1, 71, 72)",
  "The book prints a real signature, 3045022100884d..., and lists its nine elements: the byte 0x30 for a sequence, the length 0x45 of 69 bytes, then the integer R with its tag 0x02 and its length 0x21 of 33 bytes, and the integer S with its tag and its length of 32 bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The first byte of R is 0x88, which is 136; because DER reads integers as signed, any first byte of 128 or more would be read as negative, so one zero byte is put in front, and that is why R takes 33 bytes and not 32. The example adds the pieces, 2 + 32 + 1 + 2 + 32 = 69 bytes of body, 71 bytes with the two header bytes and 72 with the hash type byte, and the reader can change the first byte of R to see the padding disappear. It is like a form with a sign box that must be ticked whenever the top digit is large. The strict form of this encoding is a consensus rule since BIP66 (Wuille, 2015)."),
 ("SighashFlags", "FlagCoding", "FlagByte", "SINGLE with ANYONECANPAY is the byte 0x83",
  "The flag byte is the base value 1, 2 or 3 combined by bitwise OR with 0x80 for ANYONECANPAY, so 0x83 means SINGLE together with ANYONECANPAY.",
  "base = 3; acp = 1; flag = base + 128 * acp; (flag, flag & 3, flag & 128 != 0)", "(131, 3, True)",
  "Bitcoin Core defines SIGHASH_ALL as 1, SIGHASH_NONE as 2, SIGHASH_SINGLE as 3 and SIGHASH_ANYONECANPAY as 0x80 (Bitcoin Core, 2026), and the book says that the modifier has the value 0x80 and is applied by bitwise OR, so SINGLE with the modifier is 0x83 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The example builds the byte from a base of 3 and a switch for the modifier, giving 131, which is 0x83, and then takes it apart again with a bit mask: the two lowest bits give back the base 3 and the top bit says whether ANYONECANPAY is set. The reader can change the base to 1 or 2 or switch the modifier off. It is like a light switch panel in which the low switches choose a mode and the top switch chooses whether other people may add inputs. A node reads the byte exactly this way when it checks which parts of a transaction a signature covers."),
 ("SighashFlags", "KnownPitfalls", "SingleBugIndexes", "which inputs of 3 have no output to sign",
  "With SIGHASH_SINGLE an input whose index is not smaller than the number of outputs has no output to sign, which is the case that the legacy algorithm answers with the number one.",
  "inputs = 3; outputs = 2; (inputs > outputs, [i for i in range(inputs) if i >= outputs])", "(True, [2])",
  "The book says that SINGLE signs the one output with the same index number as the signed input (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), but a transaction can have more inputs than outputs, and for an input whose index has no matching output the legacy signature hash function returns the number one instead of a hash, a defect known as the SIGHASH_SINGLE bug, which the source of Bitcoin Core shows as a branch that returns the constant ONE when the input index is not below the number of outputs (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The example lists the inputs that run into it: with 3 inputs and 2 outputs the indexes are 0, 1 and 2, and only index 2 has no output, so the list is [2]; the reader can change the numbers and watch the list grow or vanish. It is like seating guests at numbered chairs where the third guest has no chair: the host's rule gives no answer, and the old software quietly answers with a fixed number that anyone can sign. Bitcoin Core refuses to sign in that case, as an earlier example of the chapter shows."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "HashingCost", "HashedBytesGrowth", "doubling the inputs roughly quadruples the bytes hashed",
  "If each of n signatures hashes about the whole transaction, and the transaction grows with n, the bytes hashed grow with n squared: 20 inputs cost about 3.9 times what 10 inputs cost.",
  "n = 10; per = 148; fixed = 100; h = lambda k: k * (k * per + fixed); (h(n), h(2 * n), round(h(2 * n) / h(n), 2))", "(15800, 61200, 3.87)",
  "The book warns that in the old algorithm the hash operations increase roughly quadratically with the number of inputs, so that an attacker can build a transaction that costs a node a great deal of hashing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and BIP143 states that the amount of hashing for each signature is proportional to the size of the transaction, so that hashing grows in O(n squared) with the number of signature operations, and that a reusable intermediate hash brings it to O(n) (Lau and Wuille, 2016). The example models a transaction of k inputs of an assumed 148 bytes each plus 100 fixed bytes, hashed once for every input: 10 inputs hash 15,800 bytes, 20 inputs hash 61,200 bytes, and the ratio 3.87 is close to the factor 4 that squaring predicts. It is like a teacher who rereads the whole class register for every student who signs it: doubling the class does not double the work but nearly quadruples it. The sizes are assumptions chosen for the arithmetic, not measurements."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "HashingCost", "SlowSigops", "25 seconds for 5,569 signature operations",
  "BIP143 reports that a block of 1 MB takes about 2 seconds to verify but a 1 MB transaction with 5,569 signature operations may take 25 seconds, about 4.5 milliseconds for each operation.",
  "sigops = 5569; secs = 25; block = 2; (round(secs / block, 1), round(secs * 1000 / sigops, 1))", "(12.5, 4.5)",
  "BIP143 gives the measured motivation for the new algorithm: in 2015 a 1 MB block would normally take 2 seconds to verify with an average computer, while a 1 MB transaction with 5,569 signature operations may take 25 seconds (Lau and Wuille, 2016). The example divides the two times, which shows the single transaction to be 12.5 times slower than a whole ordinary block, and divides the 25 seconds by the number of operations, which gives 4.5 milliseconds for each signature check when every check rehashes a megabyte; the reader can change the seconds or the number of operations. It is like a postal clerk who must photocopy the entire parcel before stamping each of thousands of labels. The figures belong to the computer and the year named by the BIP and say nothing about today's hardware, but the ratio explains why Bitcoin Core later capped legacy signature operations."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "ConsensusAndPolicy", "SigopCapInputs", "a cap of 2,500 operations, 2 per input",
  "If every input of a standard transaction executes 2 legacy signature operations, the cap of 2,500 allows at most 1,250 such inputs.",
  "cap = 2500; per = 2; (cap // per, cap % per)", "(1250, 0)",
  "The release notes of Bitcoin Core 30.0 say that the maximum number of potentially executed legacy signature operations in a single standard transaction is now limited to 2500, counting those in all previous output scripts, in all input scripts and in all P2SH redeem scripts (Bitcoin Core, 2026). The example turns the cap into a count of inputs: if each input executes 2 such operations, a quantity chosen only for the arithmetic, 2,500 divided by 2 gives 1,250 inputs with nothing left over, and a reader who sets 3 operations per input gets 833 with a remainder of 1. It is like a bridge with a weight limit: the limit is fixed, and how many vehicles pass depends on what each one weighs. The cap is a relay policy of the node, so a transaction above it is not relayed by default, which differs from a consensus rule that would make a block invalid."),
 ("Randomness", "SignatureSize", "LowRTries", "the chance to find a low R within 8 attempts",
  "Because a low R is a first byte below 0x80, each attempt succeeds with a chance of one half, so 8 attempts succeed with a chance of 0.9961 and at most 256 values of the counter need to be considered.",
  "tries = 8; (round(1 - 0.5 ** tries, 4), 2 ** tries)", "(0.9961, 256)",
  "Bitcoin Core signs, then checks the result with a function that returns true when the first byte of R is less than 0x80, and while it is not, it changes the extra entropy by a counter and signs again, which is the grinding for a low R (Bitcoin Core, 2026). A random first byte is below 0x80 in half the cases, so the chance that none of 8 attempts succeeds is one half to the power 8, which is 1 in 256, and the chance that at least one does is 0.9961; the reader can change the number of attempts and see how quickly certainty approaches. It is like tossing a coin until it shows heads: long runs of tails are rare, so the loop ends after about two tries on average. A low R avoids the padding zero of the previous DER example, which is why Bitcoin Core's signatures are 70 or 71 bytes and the book's sample is 72."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "SchnorrSavings", "9 bytes saved for each input of 3",
  "A schnorr signature of 64 bytes with a 32-byte key replaces an ECDSA signature of 72 bytes with a 33-byte key, which saves 9 bytes of witness for each input and 27 for 3 inputs.",
  "inputs = 3; ecdsa = 72; schnorr = 64; per = (ecdsa + 33) - (schnorr + 32); (per, per * inputs, per * inputs / 4)", "(9, 27, 6.75)",
  "BIP340 compares keeping one bit of the Y coordinate, which would give 33-byte public keys and 65-byte signatures, with encoding only the X coordinate, which gives the 32-byte public keys and 64-byte signatures that it chooses (Wuille et al., 2020), while the book's ECDSA sample takes 72 bytes with its hash type (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) and a compressed key takes 33. The example subtracts the two totals, 105 against 96 bytes, to get 9 bytes for each input, multiplies by the number of inputs, and divides by 4 because witness bytes count one weight unit each and four weight units make one virtual byte, giving 6.75 virtual bytes saved for 3 inputs; the reader can change the number of inputs or the size of the ECDSA signature. It is like swapping a long address label for a short one on every parcel of a delivery. The saving is small for one input and grows with the count, and a real ECDSA signature may be 70 to 72 bytes."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "IdentityProtocol", "FiatShamirIntegers", "one round of the identity proof with integers modulo 101",
  "With the response s = k + e x, the verifier's check s g = r + e X holds, and with a different response it would fail.",
  "n = 101; g = 7; x = 12; k = 6; e = 9; big_x = x * g % n; big_r = k * g % n; s = (k + e * x) % n; (s, s * g % n == (big_r + e * big_x) % n)", "(13, True)",
  "In the book's identification protocol Alice commits to a nonce point kG, Bob answers with a challenge e, Alice replies with s = k + e x, and Bob accepts when sG equals kG plus e times xG, which he can compute without knowing x or k (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The example plays one round in the stand-in group of integers modulo 101 with the private key 12, the nonce 6 and the challenge 9: the response is s = 13, and both sides of the check give the same number, so the answer is True. The reader can change the key, the nonce or the challenge, and the check keeps holding, because the equation is an identity; to see it fail, change s by one after it is computed. It is like a secret handshake in which only the true partner can produce the matching grip. As in the other toy examples, the real protocol runs on secp256k1, where the division that would reveal x is impossible."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Aggregation", "KeyCancellationToy", "Bob's key hides Alice's key and he signs alone",
  "If Bob announces his key as m g minus Alice's key, the sum of the two keys is m g, a key that Bob alone controls.",
  "n = 101; g = 7; a = 12; m = 5; xa = a * g % n; xm = (m * g - xa) % n; agg = (xa + xm) % n; (agg == m * g % n, agg)", "(True, 35)",
  "The book describes the key cancellation attack: Bob generates his public key as zG minus yG, so that when the two keys are combined, yG plus zG minus yG, the positive and negative terms cancel and the combined key represents only Bob's private key z, after which he can sign without Alice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The example uses the integers modulo 101: Alice's key is 12 times 7, Bob's announced key is 5 times 7 minus Alice's, and their sum is 35, which equals 5 times 7, so Bob who knows 5 controls the aggregate. The reader can change Bob's number and the check stays True, which is the point: whatever Bob picks, he can cancel Alice. It is like two people signing a joint cheque where one of them quietly swaps the account for his own. MuSig2 prevents this by weighting each key with a coefficient that depends on all the keys, as the standard states (Nick et al., 2022)."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "ThresholdSharing", "ShamirModulus", "secret 7 shared as a line modulo 101, any two shares return it",
  "A secret of 7 and a slope of 3 give the shares 10, 13 and 16 at x = 1, 2 and 3 modulo 101, and the first and third shares return the secret 7.",
  "p = 101; s = 7; a = 3; y = [(s + a * x) % p for x in (1, 2, 3)]; slope = (y[2] - y[0]) * pow(3 - 1, -1, p) % p; (y, (y[0] - slope) % p)", "([10, 13, 16], 7)",
  "Shamir's scheme hides a secret D as the value of a random polynomial at zero, over a prime larger than both the secret and the number of holders, and any k pieces recover it while k - 1 or fewer leave it completely undetermined (Shamir, 1979); the book calls the basic idea secret sharing and uses it for threshold signatures (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The example uses the line 7 + 3 x modulo 101, so the three holders receive 10, 13 and 16; holders 1 and 3 compute the slope as the difference of their values divided by the difference of their positions, which is 3, and subtract it from the first share to get back 7. The reader can change the secret, the slope or the prime. It is like a map torn into three pieces along lines so that any two overlap enough to show the treasure. The prime 101 is only for teaching, since a real secret needs a prime far larger than itself."),
 ("Randomness", "NonceSafety", "NonceReuseRecovery", "two signatures with one nonce reveal the key 17",
  "Two ECDSA signatures that use the same nonce k give k as the difference of the digests divided by the difference of the signature values, and then the private key.",
  "n = 101; d = 17; k = 29; r = 44; z1 = 11; z2 = 23; s1 = pow(k, -1, n) * (z1 + r * d) % n; s2 = pow(k, -1, n) * (z2 + r * d) % n; kk = (z1 - z2) * pow(s1 - s2, -1, n) % n; (kk, (s1 * kk - z1) * pow(r, -1, n) % n)", "(29, 17)",
  "The book says that if the same value k is used with different messages, the signing private key can be calculated by anyone, and that people have had funds stolen because of an inadvertent reuse of k, most often through an improperly initialized random-number generator (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The algebra is short: subtracting the two signature equations leaves s1 - s2 = k^-1 (z1 - z2), so k = (z1 - z2) / (s1 - s2), and from the first equation x = (s1 k - z1) / r. In the example with the private key 17 and the nonce 29 modulo 101 the attacker, who sees only the two signatures and the two digests, recovers 29 and then 17. The reader can change the key, the nonce or the digests. It is like using the same one-time pad twice: the two messages together give away what each alone would hide. The value r is taken as given here, since only its reuse matters."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "History", "PatentTermYears", "filed 1990, granted 1991: the term ends in 2010",
  "A patent filed in 1990 and granted in 1991 ran to the greater of 20 years from filing, 2010, and 17 years from grant, 2008, which is 2010.",
  "filed = 1990; granted = 1991; (max(filed + 20, granted + 17), filed + 20, granted + 17)", "(2010, 2010, 2008)",
  "Schnorr's United States patent 4,995,082 was filed on 23 February 1990 and granted on 19 February 1991 (USPTO, 1991), and the statute gives a term that ends 20 years from the date on which the application was filed (Cornell Legal Information Institute, 2026), and for patents of that age the term is the greater of the 20-year term and 17 years from grant (Cornell Legal Information Institute, 2026), which would end in 2008, so the later date, 2010, governs. Wikipedia agrees that the patent expired in February 2010 (Wikipedia, 2026). The example computes both and takes the larger, and the reader can change the years or the terms. It is like a ticket that is valid for the later of two printed expiry dates. This arithmetic explains why the book can say that the patent kept the algorithm out of open standards for almost two decades, and why a signature scheme from 1989 could enter Bitcoin only in 2021."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "TaprootSigning", "RetargetBoundary", "taproot's activation height is a multiple of 2016",
  "Block 709,632 is exactly 352 retarget periods of 2,016 blocks after the genesis block, and the first taproot spend followed it by 3 blocks.",
  "act = 709632; first = 709635; p = 2016; (act // p, act % p, first - act)", "(352, 0, 3)",
  "BIP341 states that the minimum activation height of taproot is block 709632 and that the deployment did activate at that height on Bitcoin mainnet (Wuille et al., 2020), and the record of the first taproot key-path spend in the saved mainnet data puts it in block 709,635 (Mempool Space, 2026). The example divides the activation height by the 2,016 blocks of a difficulty period, giving 352 with remainder 0, so the activation fell on a period boundary, and subtracts to find that the first spend came 3 blocks later. The reader can change the heights. It is like a school year that always begins on the first day of a term: upgrades that count in periods start where the counting turns over. The same record is checked by the chapter's earlier example that verifies this spend's signature."),
 ("SchnorrSignatures", "Bip340Format", "TaggedPrefixBlock", "two hashes of the tag fill one 64-byte hash block",
  "BIP340 prefixes the data with the hash of the tag twice, which is 64 bytes and exactly one block of SHA-256, so an implementation can precompute it.",
  "import hashlib; reps = 2; h = hashlib.sha256(b'BIP0340/challenge').digest(); (len(h * reps), len(h * reps) % 64 == 0)", "(64, True)",
  "BIP340 includes the tag in a hash by prefixing the data with SHA256(tag) twice, and it explains that this is a 64-byte context-specific constant and that the block size of SHA-256 is also 64 bytes, so optimized implementations can start from a precomputed state (Wuille et al., 2020). The example hashes the tag BIP0340/challenge, the one used for the challenge of a signature, repeats the 32-byte result the chosen number of times, and tests whether the length is a whole number of 64-byte blocks: with 2 repetitions the length is 64 and the answer is True, and with 3 it is 96 and the answer is False, which the reader can try. It is like a stamp that always fills exactly one page of a notebook, so that the page can be stamped in advance. The prefix also separates the hashes of different purposes, so a challenge hash can never be mistaken for another kind of hash."),
]
